# Quickstart

This is the offline capabilities tour: see everything herold does on one
machine, with no DNS and no internet. You run a single container, bind it to
loopback ports, and exercise the mail, calendar, contacts, and chat surfaces
against local accounts on a made-up domain.

It uses SQLite for storage and the self-signed certificate baked into the
image. There is no public DNS, no ACME, and no smart host. The posture is
appropriate for trying the software on a single host and nothing else; for a
real deployment with a real domain see _Going Live_.

## What you need

- Docker, or any OCI-compatible runtime such as Podman.
- A web browser.
- Optionally, a desktop IMAP client (Thunderbird, Apple Mail, mutt, ...) if you
  want to exercise the IMAP and SMTP submission surfaces too.

## 1. Start the container (ephemeral)

```bash
docker run --rm --name herold \
  -p 127.0.0.1:1025:1025 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:1587:1587 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:1143:1143 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:1993:1993 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:9443:9443 \
  ghcr.io/hanshuebner/herold:latest
```

This pulls the latest published image and starts the server with storage
inside the container. The image ships a baked-in `system.toml` at
`/etc/herold/system.toml` and runs as a nonroot user.

The published ports are: `1025` SMTP relay, `1587` SMTP submission (STARTTLS),
`1143` IMAP (STARTTLS), `1993` IMAPS (implicit TLS), `8080` public HTTP /
Suite SPA, `9443` admin HTTP / admin SPA. All bound to `127.0.0.1` so the
surface stays on the local host.

`docker stop herold` discards every mailbox, principal, and config change you
made. That is fine for a tour; see [Persistent state](#11-persistent-state)
below if you want to keep what you build.

## 2. Bootstrap the first admin principal

In a second terminal:

```bash
docker exec herold /usr/local/bin/herold bootstrap \
  --email admin@example.local --password 'change-me-now'
```

The command prints the admin email, the password, and a `hk_...` API key. The
API key is also written to `~/.herold/credentials.toml` inside the container
(at `/home/nonroot/.herold/credentials.toml` under the nonroot user), with a
`server_url` derived from the `kind = "admin"` listener. Subsequent
`docker exec herold ...` CLI calls read both values from that file.

Passing `--password` on the command line is acceptable for this loopback-only
tour. For any non-throwaway installation, omit `--password` (a random one is
generated and printed once) or supply it via stdin or the
`HEROLD_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` environment variable.

## 3. Add a local domain

```bash
docker exec herold /usr/local/bin/herold domain add example.local
```

`example.local` is not a real domain and does not need to be. The relay
listener accepts mail for hosted domains and delivers it directly into the
recipient's INBOX, so everything in this tour stays on the loopback.

## 4. Create a second principal

So you can send mail between two accounts, create a second principal:

```bash
docker exec herold /usr/local/bin/herold principal create \
  alice@example.local --password 'change-me-now'
```

You now have two accounts on `example.local`: `admin@example.local` and
`alice@example.local`.

## 5. Drop a test message into the inbox

It helps to have something to read before you sign in. The SMTP relay listener
at `127.0.0.1:1025` accepts mail for local domains and delivers it directly
into the recipient's INBOX. This one-liner uses only `nc` and ships a
plain-text message addressed entirely within `example.local`:

```bash
{
  printf 'EHLO localhost\r\n'
  printf 'MAIL FROM:<alice@example.local>\r\n'
  printf 'RCPT TO:<admin@example.local>\r\n'
  printf 'DATA\r\n'
  printf 'From: Quickstart Test <alice@example.local>\r\n'
  printf 'To: admin@example.local\r\n'
  printf 'Subject: herold quickstart test\r\n'
  printf 'Date: %s\r\n' "$(date -u +'%a, %d %b %Y %H:%M:%S +0000')"
  printf '\r\n'
  printf 'Hello from the quickstart smoke test.\r\n'
  printf '.\r\n'
  printf 'QUIT\r\n'
} | nc -w 2 127.0.0.1 1025
```

Each line should come back with a `2xx` status.

## 6. Open the Suite web client

Open `http://localhost:8080/` in a browser. The Suite SPA loads and
immediately tries to fetch `/.well-known/jmap`. With no session cookie present
the SPA renders its own inline login form.

Sign in as `admin@example.local` with the password from step 2. The SPA posts
JSON to `/api/v1/auth/login`; on success the server sets the
`herold_public_session` cookie (HttpOnly, `Path=/`) plus `herold_public_csrf`
(non-HttpOnly so the SPA's JS reads it and attaches `X-CSRF-Token` on every
mutating request). The SPA re-runs its JMAP bootstrap and lands on `/#/mail`.

From here:

- Read the test message from step 5 in the inbox.
- Compose a message to `alice@example.local`. The outbound queue checks the
  recipient's domain, sees that `example.local` is hosted by this herold
  instance, and ingests the message locally instead of attempting to
  MX-resolve a domain that does not exist on the public internet. Sign out and
  back in as `alice@example.local` to see it arrive.
- Calendar (`/#/calendar`), contacts (`/#/contacts`), and chat (`/#/chat`) are
  reachable in the same SPA shell. Chat is on by default; DMs and Spaces ride
  the same JMAP store as mail, and presence, typing, reactions, and 1:1 video
  calls flow over the WebSocket at `/chat/ws` with no extra configuration.

Self-service settings live at `/#/settings` (or press `g s`): password change,
two-factor authentication enrolment, and API-key management.

## 7. Open the operator UI

Visit `http://localhost:9443/admin/` on the admin listener. The Svelte admin
SPA renders its own login form; sign in with the bootstrap credentials and the
SPA posts JSON to `/api/v1/auth/login`. On success the server sets the
`herold_admin_session` cookie (HttpOnly, `Path=/`) and `herold_admin_csrf`
(non-HttpOnly so the SPA sends `X-CSRF-Token` on mutating requests).

The operator UI is where you see the server's state: hosted domains, the
principals you created, the outbound queue, and the audit log. On this
loopback tour the admin listener speaks plain HTTP; in production it is
loopback-only or fronted by a reverse proxy.

## 8. (Optional) Connect a desktop IMAP/SMTP client

Herold also exposes IMAP and SMTP submission for desktop clients:

- Server: `localhost`
- IMAP+STARTTLS port: `1143` (or IMAPS port `1993` for implicit TLS)
- SMTP submission port: `1587` (with STARTTLS and the same username/password).
  Do not use port `1025` for outgoing mail in a client - that listener is the
  inbound relay and does not require AUTH.
- Username: `admin@example.local` (or `alice@example.local`)
- Password: the one you set when creating the principal

The TLS certificate baked into the image is self-signed and shared by every
container built from the same image tag. Apple Mail and Thunderbird will
prompt to accept it the first time you connect; click through and check
"always trust" so the prompt does not reappear. The baked-in cert covers
`mail.example.local`, `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, and `::1` in its SAN.

A note on `localhost` vs `127.0.0.1`: macOS resolves `localhost` to the IPv6
loopback (`::1`) first, and `docker run -p 127.0.0.1:...` only binds the IPv4
loopback on the host side. If your client cannot connect to `localhost` but
works against `127.0.0.1`, that is why - either use the literal `127.0.0.1` in
your client, or publish the port on both stacks
(`-p 127.0.0.1:1143:1143 -p [::1]:1143:1143`).

## 9. Persistent state

The ephemeral run in step 1 stores the SQLite DB, blobs, and FTS index inside
the container's writable layer; `docker stop herold` discards all of it. To
keep state across restarts, mount a host directory (or named volume) at
`/var/lib/herold`:

```bash
mkdir -p ./herold-data
docker run -d --name herold \
  -v "$(pwd)/herold-data:/var/lib/herold" \
  -p 127.0.0.1:1025:1025 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:1587:1587 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:1143:1143 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:1993:1993 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:9443:9443 \
  ghcr.io/hanshuebner/herold:latest
```

The host directory must be writable by UID `65532` (the distroless `nonroot`
user that herold runs as):

```bash
sudo chown -R 65532:65532 ./herold-data
```

To override the baked-in `system.toml` (for example to switch to Postgres or
bind real ports), bind-mount your own file at `/etc/herold/system.toml`.

## 10. Where to next

This tour stays on one machine with a made-up domain. To go further:

- For a production deployment with a real domain, public inbound, MX records,
  ACME-issued certificates, DKIM, and MTA-STS, follow _Going Live_ in the
  admin manual.
- To front the Suite and operator UIs with a reverse proxy and a real TLS
  certificate, see _Reverse Proxy and TLS_.
- For the full `system.toml` schema and every tunable, see
  _Configuration Reference_.
